//? Title: Hello, nn: The Build, the Kernels and Their Limits
//? Subtitle: What the library is built with, why a matrix product runs at a hundred times the speed of a loop, how float32 rounding grows with the inner dimension, and a first model of Old Faithful, using datamunge's nn::build_info
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
The first program in any numerical library asks what is there: which types, which backends, how many threads, how fast. This
report answers that for `datamunge::nn` with the new `nn::build_info()`, then asks what the numbers mean. A matrix product does
\(2n^3\) operations on \(3n^2\) numbers, so it can be limited by arithmetic rather than by memory. The library's float32 GEMM reaches about 160 to 170 GFLOP/s at \(n=1024\). A unit-stride triple loop reaches a fifth to a twentieth of that,
depending on how well the compiler vectorises it. A cache-hostile one falls to about 2 GFLOP/s, and an elementwise addition, limited by memory traffic, stays far below the GEMM. A float32 product's rounding
error stays far inside its worst-case bound \(ku\) as the inner dimension \(k\) grows to 16 384. Scaled by the random-walk size \(u\sqrt{\sum(ab)^2}\), it grows like \(\sqrt k\) up to \(k=256\) and then
much more slowly, the signature of a blocked kernel. As a first model, least squares for Old Faithful through `nn::solve` matches `stats::LM` to
\(7\times10^{-14}\) in float64. In float32 it is off by \(1.3\times10^{-4}\), as the condition number 159 of \(X^\top X\) predicts. The library gained `nn::build_info`, `nn::BuildInfo` and `nn::matmul_flops`.
)

\toc

> The problem

TensorFlow's "hello" example lists the framework's devices (CPUs and GPUs) and prints a constant computed in a session.
`datamunge::nn` runs on the CPU only, with no sessions and no device placement: an operation runs when it is called. The useful first
questions are therefore different. What was this build compiled with, which BLAS and LAPACK does it call, and how many threads may it use? And do its kernels run as
fast and as accurately as they should?

This report replaces the original example `examples/nn/0_foundations/0.1_tensors_graphs_sessions/hello_tf.cpp`. That example also
queried NVIDIA's management library for GPU details. That part reports hardware, not the library, and is not repeated here. The build
facts it printed by hand are now `nn::build_info()`.

> The method

>> Counting the work of a matrix product

The product \(C=AB\) of an \([m,k]\) and a \([k,n]\) matrix has \(mn\) entries, each a sum of \(k\) products: \(mk n\) multiplications and \(mkn\) additions, so
`nn::matmul_flops(m, k, n)` \(=2mkn\). Throughput is flops divided by time, in GFLOP/s. A core's peak is its clock rate times the flops per cycle. With
fused multiply-add on 16-wide float32 vectors (AVX-512) and two vector units, that is 64 flops per cycle, about 200 GFLOP/s per core at 3 GHz. Float64 vectors hold half as many numbers, so
the peak halves.

>> Arithmetic intensity

The *arithmetic intensity* of a kernel is flops per byte of memory traffic. For square float32 matrices, the product reads and writes at least \(3n^2\) numbers
(\(12n^2\) bytes) and does \(2n^3\) flops, an intensity of \(n/6\) flops per byte, which grows with \(n\). Elementwise addition does \(n^2\) flops on \(12n^2\) bytes,
\(1/12\) per byte, at every size. Memory delivers some tens of bytes per nanosecond, so a kernel with intensity well below about ten flops per byte runs at the speed of memory, and one above it can run at
the speed of arithmetic (the roofline model, Williams, Waterman and Patterson, 2009). A product reaches that speed only if each number loaded from memory is reused
about \(n\) times while it sits in registers and cache. That is what blocked GEMM kernels arrange (Goto and van de Geijn, 2008), and what a textbook triple
loop does not. The loop order matters too. With \(i\)-\(j\)-\(k\) the inner loop walks down a column of \(B\), a stride of \(n\) numbers, and every step can miss the cache.
With \(i\)-\(k\)-\(j\) it walks along rows of \(B\) and \(C\) at unit stride, which the compiler can vectorise.

>> Rounding in a product

Each entry \(c_{ij}=\sum_{l=1}^ka_{il}b_{lj}\) is a sum of \(k\) rounded products. By the analysis of the custom-kernel report (`custom_op_kernel`), computing it in floating point with
unit roundoff \(u\) gives

$$
\lvert\hat c_{ij}-c_{ij}\rvert\le\gamma_k\sum_{l=1}^k\lvert a_{il}b_{lj}\rvert,\qquad\gamma_k\approx ku,
$$
\alttext(The absolute error of the computed entry c i j is at most gamma k times the sum over l of the absolute products a i l times b l j, with gamma k about k u.)

whatever the order of summation (Higham, 2002, §3.5). For random signs the errors partly cancel. With one sequential accumulator their typical size is about
\(u\sqrt k\) times the root-mean-square partial sum, and that partial sum grows like \(\sqrt{\sum_l(a_{il}b_{lj})^2}\cdot\sqrt k\). A blocked kernel that keeps many short partial sums, and adds the partial sums
at the end, has fewer roundings per term and a smaller error. Its scaled error then grows more slowly than \(\sqrt k\).

>> Conditioning of least squares

The least-squares coefficients solve the normal equations \(X^\top X\beta=X^\top y\). A perturbation of relative size \(u\) in \(X^\top X\) moves \(\beta\) by a relative amount up to about
\(\kappa(X^\top X)\,u\), where \(\kappa\) is the condition number, the ratio of the largest to the smallest eigenvalue. In float32 a condition number of 100 to 1000
therefore costs two or three of the seven available digits.

> Simple examples

>> The build, and the first tensors

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;

int main() {
  std::print("{}\n", nn::build_info().to_markdown());
  // Hello, tensors: values exist as soon as the call returns; there is no session to run.
  const Tensor a = nn::tensor({{1, 2}, {3, 4}}), b = nn::tensor({{5, 6}, {7, 8}});
  std::print("- a + b = {}\n", (a + b).values_str());
  std::print("- a @ b = {} ({} flops)\n", nn::matmul(a, b).values_str(), nn::matmul_flops(2, 2, 2));
  std::print("- mean(a) = {}, a cast to int32 = {} ({})\n", nn::mean(a).item(), nn::cast(a, nn::DType::Int32).values_str(),
             nn::dtype_name(nn::cast(a, nn::DType::Int32).dtype()));
}
```
