| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 32 | 6.55e+04 | 58.4 | 35.2 | 17.04 | 6.12 | 1.97 |
| 64 | 5.24e+05 | 97.6 | 46.8 | 18.04 | 5.40 | 4.44 |
| 128 | 4.19e+06 | 110.3 | 53.0 | 23.68 | 3.26 | 9.08 |
| 256 | 3.36e+07 | 119.3 | 56.9 | 24.39 | 2.55 | 3.39 |
| 512 | 2.68e+08 | 116.5 | 56.2 | 23.29 | 1.65 | 6.38 |
| 1024 | 2.15e+09 | 112.3 | 54.6 | 16.06 | not run | 14.86 |

Arithmetic intensity at n = 1024: matmul 171 flops per byte of its inputs and output, elementwise add 0.083.
\image(figures/hello_tf_gemm.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Throughput against matrix size, both on log scales. Blue and green: the library GEMM in float32 and float64. Orange and red: textbook triple loops in the \(i\)-\(k\)-\(j\) and \(i\)-\(j\)-\(k\) orders. Grey: elementwise addition, counted as one flop per element.)
\alttext(A log-log plot over log2 n from 5 to 10. The blue float32 GEMM curve rises from about 83 to 168 GFLOP slash s, the green float64 curve from about 49 to 81. The orange i-k-j loop stays flat, well below the GEMM. The red i-j-k loop falls to about 2 at n equals 512. The grey addition curve rises from about 2 to 23, with a jump between n equals 256 and 512.)

The measurements follow the model.

- The library GEMM approaches its plateau by \(n=128\), at about 160 to 170 GFLOP/s in float32 and half that in float64, as the vector widths predict.
- The \(i\)-\(k\)-\(j\) loop holds a constant rate at every size, several times slower than the GEMM: it vectorises, but it reloads \(B\) from memory for every row of \(C\).
  Its rate depends on the compiler's vectorisation. Builds of this report have measured from 9 to 33 GFLOP/s.
- The \(i\)-\(j\)-\(k\) loop does the same arithmetic in the cache-hostile order. It falls to about 2 GFLOP/s at \(n=512\) as the column stride outgrows the caches.
- Elementwise addition does \(1/12\) flop per byte against the GEMM's \(n/6\) (171 at \(n=1024\)). It stays an order of magnitude below the GEMM even at its best.

The jump in addition between \(n=256\) and 512 is the library's parallel threshold. Kernels with fewer than 65 536 elements run on one thread,
because starting threads would cost more than the work. Absolute numbers depend on the machine. The orders of magnitude between the curves are the stable part.

>> Accuracy: float32 products against the bound

The program multiplies \(64\times k\) by \(k\times64\) float32 matrices of standard normal entries, for inner dimensions \(k=16\) to 16 384. It compares every one of the 4096 entries
with the float64 product of the same entries.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hello_tf_accuracy.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  nn::NoGradGuard guard;
  nn::Generator gen(2);
  // Rounding error of a float32 product C = A B with inner dimension k, against the float64 product
  // of the same (float32-representable) entries. Per entry, |error| <= gamma_k sum_l |a_il b_lj|.
  const double u = std::ldexp(1.0, -24);
  std::print("| k | median relative error | largest relative error | median error / (u √Σ(ab)²) | worst-case bound k u |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> lk, med, worst, wmed;
  for (int k : {16, 64, 256, 1024, 4096, 16384}) {
    const Tensor A = nn::normal({64, k}, 0, 1, gen), B = nn::normal({k, 64}, 0, 1, gen);
    const auto c32 = nn::matmul(A, B).to_vector<float>();
    const auto c64 = nn::matmul(nn::cast(A, nn::DType::Float64), nn::cast(B, nn::DType::Float64)).to_vector<double>();
    const auto scale = nn::matmul(nn::abs(nn::cast(A, nn::DType::Float64)), nn::abs(nn::cast(B, nn::DType::Float64))).to_vector<double>();
    // The random-walk scale of the rounding errors: sqrt(sum_l (a_il b_lj)^2).
    const auto rms = nn::sqrt(nn::matmul(nn::square(nn::cast(A, nn::DType::Float64)), nn::square(nn::cast(B, nn::DType::Float64)))).to_vector<double>();
    std::vector<double> walk(c32.size());
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < walk.size(); ++i) walk[i] = std::abs(c32[i] - c64[i]) / (u * rms[i]);
    std::ranges::sort(walk);
    std::vector<double> rel(c32.size());
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < rel.size(); ++i) rel[i] = std::abs(c32[i] - c64[i]) / scale[i];  // error relative to sum |a b|
    std::ranges::sort(rel);
    lk.push_back(std::log2(k));
    med.push_back(rel[rel.size() / 2]);
    worst.push_back(rel.back());
    wmed.push_back(walk[walk.size() / 2]);
    std::print("| {} | {:.2e} | {:.2e} | {:.2f} | {:.2e} |\n", k, med.back(), worst.back(), walk[walk.size() / 2], k * u);
  }
  // Left: error relative to the bound's scale sum |a b|. Right: in units of u sqrt(sum (a b)^2), against
  // the sqrt(k) growth a single sequential accumulator would show.
  auto lg = [](std::vector<double> v) { for (auto& e : v) e = std::log10(e); return v; };
  auto left = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::vector<double> bk{4, 14}, bound{std::log10(16 * u), std::log10(16384 * u)};
  left.lines(bk, bound, "bound k u", dm::graphics::RGB{252, 165, 165}, 1.5);
  left.lines(lk, lg(worst), "largest of 4096 entries", dm::graphics::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2.0).points(lk, lg(worst), "", dm::graphics::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 5.0);
  left.lines(lk, lg(med), "median", dm::graphics::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0).points(lk, lg(med), "", dm::graphics::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 5.0);
  left.x_limits(4, 14).y_limits(-9, -2).title("Error / Σ|a b|").x_label("log2 k (inner dimension)").y_label("log10 relative error");
  auto right = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::vector<double> sq{std::log10(wmed[0]), std::log10(wmed[0]) + 0.5 * std::log10(16384.0 / 16)};
  right.lines(bk, sq, "√k growth", dm::graphics::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1.5);
  right.lines(lk, lg(wmed), "median", dm::graphics::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0).points(lk, lg(wmed), "", dm::graphics::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 5.0);
  right.x_limits(4, 14).y_limits(-0.5, 2.0).title("Error / (u √Σ(a b)²)").x_label("log2 k (inner dimension)").y_label("log10 scaled error");
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  plot.add(left);
  plot.add(right);
  plot.size(1100, 440).fit_panels();
  plot.save_svg("build/hello_tf_accuracy.svg");
}
```
