| 16384 | 2.70e-09 | 1.85e-08 | 3.69 | 9.77e-04 |
\image(figures/hello_tf_accuracy.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Rounding error of float32 matrix products against the inner dimension \(k\). Left: relative to \(\sum_l\lvert a_{il}b_{lj}\rvert\), the median (blue) and largest (red) over 4096 entries, with the worst-case bound \(ku\) (pale red). Right: the median in units of \(u\sqrt{\sum_l(a_{il}b_{lj})^2}\) (blue), with a line of slope one half through its first point (grey), the \(\sqrt k\) growth of a single sequential accumulator.)
\alttext(Left: the pale red bound rises from 10 to the minus 6 at k equals 16 to 10 to the minus 3 at k equals 16384; the red largest-error curve stays between 10 to the minus 6.8 and 10 to the minus 7.7, falling after k equals 256; the blue median stays near 10 to the minus 8 and falls to 10 to the minus 8.6. Right: the blue median rises along the grey slope-one-half line from 0.62 at k equals 16 to 2.3 at k equals 256, then bends below it, reaching 3.7 at k equals 16384 where the grey line is at 20.)

Every entry is inside the worst-case bound, by a factor of 5 at \(k=16\) and of 50 000 at \(k=16\,384\) for the largest entry. The bound grows like \(k\). The errors relative to
\(\sum\lvert ab\rvert\) instead *fall* as \(k\) grows, because cancellation makes the sum much smaller than the sum of absolute values. On the random-walk scale (right) the median
error grows like \(\sqrt k\), as one sequential accumulator would, up to \(k=256\), and then almost stops growing: 2.3 at \(k=256\) and 3.7 at \(k=16\,384\), where a single accumulator would reach
about 20. The kernel evidently sums blocks of a few hundred terms and then combines the blocks, which is the pairwise effect of the custom-kernel report.

> A dataset: least squares for Old Faithful

The first model is the oldest one. For 272 eruptions of the Old Faithful geyser, the waiting time to the next eruption is fitted as a straight
line in the length of the current one, by solving the normal equations with `nn::solve` in both precisions and comparing with `stats::LM`.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hello_tf_faithful.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  // Old Faithful: waiting time to the next eruption (min) against eruption length (min), 272 eruptions.
  const auto geyser = dm::datasets::faithful();
  const auto x = geyser.get_column<double>("eruptions").values(), y = geyser.get_column<double>("waiting").values();
  const std::int64_t n = static_cast<std::int64_t>(x.size());
  const auto lm = dm::stats::LM(dm::formula("waiting ~ eruptions"), geyser).coefficients();
  // The same fit with nn: the normal equations X^T X beta = X^T y, in float32 and float64.
  std::vector<double> design;
  for (double e : x) design.push_back(1.0), design.push_back(e);
  std::print("| precision | intercept | slope | largest error against stats::LM |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (auto dt : {nn::DType::Float32, nn::DType::Float64}) {
    const Tensor X = nn::from_vector(design, {n, 2}).to(dt), Y = nn::from_vector(y, {n, 1}).to(dt);
    const Tensor Xt = nn::transpose(X);
    const auto beta = nn::solve(nn::matmul(Xt, X), nn::matmul(Xt, Y)).to_vector<double>();
    std::print("| {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.1e} |\n", nn::dtype_name(dt), beta[0], beta[1],
               std::max(std::abs(beta[0] - lm[0]), std::abs(beta[1] - lm[1])));
  }
  const Tensor X = nn::from_vector(design, {n, 2});
  const auto ev = nn::eigvalsh(nn::matmul(nn::transpose(X), X)).to_vector<double>();
  std::print("\nstats::LM: waiting = {:.6f} + {:.6f} eruptions. Condition number of XᵀX: {:.1f}; κ u in float32 = {:.1e}.\n",
             lm[0], lm[1], ev[1] / ev[0], ev[1] / ev[0] * std::ldexp(1.0, -24));

  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.points(x, y, "eruptions", dm::graphics::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 4.0);
  plot.lines({1.5, 5.2}, {lm[0] + 1.5 * lm[1], lm[0] + 5.2 * lm[1]}, "least squares", dm::graphics::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0);
  plot.x_limits(1.5, 5.2).y_limits(40, 100).title("Old Faithful").x_label("eruption length (min)").y_label("waiting time to the next (min)");
  plot.size(1100, 440);
  plot.save_svg("build/hello_tf_faithful.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| precision | intercept | slope | largest error against stats::LM |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| float32 | 33.474529 | 10.729604 | 1.3e-04 |
| float64 | 33.474397 | 10.729641 | 6.9e-14 |

stats::LM: waiting = 33.474397 + 10.729641 eruptions. Condition number of XᵀX: 159.1; κ u in float32 = 9.5e-06.
\image(figures/hello_tf_faithful.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Waiting time to the next eruption against eruption length for 272 eruptions of Old Faithful, with the least-squares line.)
\alttext(A scatter of grey points in two clusters: short eruptions of about 1.6 to 2.5 minutes followed by waits of 45 to 65 minutes, and long eruptions of 3.5 to 5 minutes followed by waits of 70 to 95 minutes. A blue line rises from about 49.6 at 1.5 minutes to 89.3 at 5.2 minutes.)

Each extra minute of eruption adds 10.73 minutes to the wait, from an intercept of 33.47 minutes. In float64 the normal equations reproduce `stats::LM` to
\(7\times10^{-14}\). In float32 they are off by \(1.3\times10^{-4}\) in the intercept, a relative error of \(4\times10^{-6}\), close to the predicted \(\kappa u=9.5\times10^{-6}\) with \(\kappa=159\). The condition number is that
large because eruption lengths, 1.6 to 5.1 minutes, are far from zero, so the columns for the intercept and the slope are nearly parallel.
Centring the predictor would reduce \(\kappa\), as it did for gradient descent in the automatic-differentiation report (`autodiff`). Float32 is enough for training a network,
where gradients are noisy anyway, but not for reporting coefficients to six digits from an ill-conditioned system.

> Conclusion

`nn::build_info()` replaces TensorFlow's device listing with the facts that matter for a CPU library: the BLAS and LAPACK in use, the threads,
the compiler and the CPU's vector features. The measurements then showed what those facts buy. A matrix product's arithmetic intensity grows
with \(n\), so a blocked GEMM can run at the speed of arithmetic: about 160 to 170 GFLOP/s here, 5 to 20 times a vectorised triple loop and 70 or more times a cache-hostile
one. An elementwise operation's intensity is fixed and small, so it runs at the speed of memory. Float32 products stay far inside their worst-case
rounding bound, and a blocked kernel keeps the typical error from growing like \(\sqrt k\). The first model, least squares in float32, lost the digits the condition number predicted.
For a CPU-only library these are the "devices": cores, vector units, caches and memory bandwidth, and the precision of the type chosen.

